---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const phaseCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '0.35*(x - 0.01)', label: 'solid–liquid boundary (melting)', color: '#1d4ed8' },
    { expr: '0.02 + 0.12*log(x/0.006)', label: 'liquid–gas boundary (boiling)', color: '#d9480f', width: 2.2 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0.006, max: 0.04, label: 'pressure (schematic)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 1, label: 'temperature (schematic)' },
  crossings: true,
  height: 340,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/foundational/states-of-matter">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Look at a glass of iced water and you are seeing all three states of matter at once — a
    solid cube, a liquid, and the gas bubbling off its surface. The particle model explains
    all three with one idea: matter is made of particles in constant motion, and the state is
    decided by a tug-of-war between two things — <strong>how much kinetic energy the
    particles have</strong> (set by temperature) and <strong>how strongly they attract each
    other</strong> (set by the substance and the pressure). Solid, liquid, gas — three answers
    to one question: <strong>how tightly do the particles cling to each other?</strong> Strong
    attraction with fixed positions = solid; strong attraction with mobile positions =
    liquid; barely any attraction = gas. Adding energy weakens the grip, so matter moves
    between states — and a pressure–temperature map (a <em>phase diagram</em>) shows exactly
    which state wins where.
  </p>
  <p>
    The model earns its keep because it explains things you can observe with your own senses:
    why a smell crosses a room even with no breeze, why sweat cools you, why ice floats,
    why a pressure cooker cooks faster, and why water behaves like almost no other substance
    on the graph below.
  </p>

  <h2>Solids, liquids and gases: the three arrangements</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Solids</strong> hold their shape because their particles are locked into fixed
    positions — an ordered lattice — and only <em>vibrate</em> about those positions. Heat a
    solid and the vibrations grow more energetic until, at the melting point, they are strong
    enough to break the fixed positions: the particles keep touching but can now slide, which
    is <strong>melting</strong>. <strong>Liquids</strong> therefore keep a fixed volume (the
    particles are still in contact) but take the shape of their container, because the
    arrangement is disordered and mobile. Heat further and at the boiling point particles
    break contact entirely: <strong>boiling</strong> (or quietly, at any temperature, from the
    surface: <strong>evaporation</strong>). <strong>Gases</strong> have particles flying
    freely — at room temperature, nitrogen molecules travel at ~500 m/s — with almost no
    mutual grip, which is why a gas expands to fill any container, can be compressed (mostly
    empty space between particles), and exerts pressure by billions of microscopic collisions
    per second on every wall.
  </p>
  <p>
    The model makes testable predictions: gases are a thousand times less dense than their
    liquids (water vapour at 100 °C is about 1/1,700 the density of liquid water) because the
    particles are the same but the spacing is not; and <strong>convection</strong> works in
    liquids and gases (mobile particles carry heat by travelling) but never in solids, where
    heat can only crawl by vibration (<strong>conduction</strong>).
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the particles themselves expand when things get hot."
    They do not — a steel ball heated in a flame still contains the same-sized iron atoms.
    What expands is the <em>spacing</em>: hotter particles vibrate harder and push their
    neighbours apart. Railway gaps, bridge expansion joints and the wobbly fit of a tight jar
    lid after hot water all live on this fact.
  </p>

  <h2>State changes and their energy bookkeeping</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the observation that defeats most beginners: <strong>energy goes in even without
    the temperature rising</strong>. Melt ice and it stays pinned at 0 °C until the last cube
    is gone, even though the burner pours in heat the whole time. The energy is not vanishing
    — it is <em>latent heat</em> ("hidden heat"), used not to speed particles up but to pull
    their attractions apart: reorganising the lattice costs 334 J per gram of ice. Boiling
    costs far more, about 2,260 J/g, because separating particles completely (liquid → gas)
    breaks far more attractions than merely loosening positions (solid → liquid). The
    bookkeeping runs both ways: when steam condenses on a window or vapour freezes as frost,
    the same energy is released — which is why a steam burn is so much worse than a
    boiling-water burn, and why sweating works so well: each gram of sweat evaporating
    carries ~2,260 J away from your skin.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Boiling, specifically, is a pressure phenomenon.</strong> A liquid boils when the
    pressure of the vapour it can generate matches the pressure pressing down on it from
    outside. So the boiling point depends on the surrounding pressure: 100 °C at sea level,
    ~71 °C on Everest's summit (pressure ≈ one third of sea level — mountaineers can't make
    decent tea), and ~121 °C in a pressure cooker at roughly double atmospheric pressure —
    which is exactly why pressure cookers cook faster: hotter water, not harder water.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="a schematic phase map — the two boundaries meet at the triple point where all three states coexist" widget="plotter" config={phaseCfg} />

  <p>
    The widget shows the general shape of a <strong>phase diagram</strong>: every point is a
    pressure–temperature combination, the blue line is where solid and liquid trade places,
    and the orange line is where liquid and gas do. The crossing is the <strong>triple
    point</strong> — for water, at 0.01 °C and 611 Pa (about 0.6% of atmospheric pressure),
    all three phases share a flask in stable coexistence, the fixed reference from which the
    kelvin is defined. Below that pressure, liquid water cannot exist at all: ice sublimates
    straight to vapour — which is why Martian water skips the liquid phase (the atmosphere is
    thinner than the triple-point pressure), and why your frost-free freezer slowly shrinks
    ice cubes left in it.
  </p>

  <h2>Heating curves: the bookkeeping drawn as a graph</h2>
  <p>
    The latent-heat story has a picture: heat crushed ice steadily and plot temperature
    against time. The graph is a staircase. A rising line while ice warms (−20 °C → 0 °C);
    a <em>flat</em> line at 0 °C for the whole melt (energy in, temperature pinned — the
    334 J/g of fusion being spent ungluing the lattice); a steeper rising line while
    liquid water warms (liquid water's specific heat, 4.2 J/g per °C, is unusually high —
    another of water's signature properties); a long flat line at 100 °C for the boil
    (2,260 J/g of vaporisation — the longest step); then rising vapour. Two flat steps,
    one per phase change, and their lengths are the latent heats to scale. The same curve
    read backwards is the cooling curve chemists use to test purity: a pure substance
    freezes at a single sharp temperature (one flat step), while mixtures slide through a
    range (a slope) — the reason a melting point is a standard identity check in every
    chemistry lab.
  </p>
  <p>
    Gas behaviour closes the loop. Compress a gas and pressure rises because the same
    number of collisions now land on less wall; heat it at fixed volume and pressure rises
    because the particles hit harder and more often — the two everyday observations
    (a bike pump warming, a sealed can exploding on a stove) that the kinetic theory
    unifies, and the qualitative face of the gas laws the Going deeper section makes
    quantitative.
  </p>

  <h2>Diffusion: the random walk that spreads everything</h2>
  <p>
    The second syllabus topic is the one you can smell. <strong>Diffusion</strong> is the
    spreading of particles from where they are concentrated to where they are not — and the
    particle model explains it with no exotic machinery at all. Gas and liquid particles move
    <em>randomly</em>; if more of them start on one side, random motion alone guarantees a net
    drift away from the crowded side (more particles leaving than arriving), until the
    spread is even. Open a perfume bottle at the front of a quiet room and, a minute later,
    the back row can smell it — no fan, no current, just billions of collisions redirecting
    molecules on a random walk. Drop a tea bag in hot water and watch the colour bleed
    outward on its own; drop potassium permanganate crystals into still water and the purple
    front advances for hours.
  </p>
  <p>
    Diffusion is also the transport system of life: oxygen diffuses from your lungs'
    alveoli into the blood (a distance of about one micrometre), nutrients from your gut,
    and carbon dioxide back out — every exchange surface in biology is thin precisely because
    diffusion only works well over short distances (it slows with the square of the distance:
    ten times further takes a hundred times longer).
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>rate</strong> follows two clean rules. <strong>Mass:</strong> lighter
    particles move faster at the same temperature — the classic demonstration is the ring of
    ammonium chloride forming inside a tube when ammonia (17 g/mol) meets hydrogen chloride
    (36.5 g/mol) from opposite ends: the white ring forms about 1.45× closer to the heavier
    gas, roughly the inverse square root of the mass ratio. <strong>Temperature:</strong>
    hotter particles move faster and collide more often, so diffusion speeds up — which is
    why sugar dissolves and spreads so much faster in hot tea, and why a fridge slows
    bacterial spread as well as bacterial growth.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The quantitative skeleton under all of this is the <strong>kinetic theory of gases</strong>:
    pressure is nothing but molecular collisions, and working through the mechanics yields the
    ideal gas law, pV = nRT — the same n (moles) you will meet in the stoichiometry lesson.
    The theory also explains <strong>Brownian motion</strong>: pollen grains jittering in
    water because they are being bombarded unevenly by invisible molecules — the 1905
    analysis (by Einstein) of exactly this jitter was among the first quantitative proofs
    that atoms exist, settling a debate then still live. Temperature itself gets a precise
    meaning here: it is proportional to the average kinetic energy of the particles, which is
    why there is a floor — absolute zero (0 K, −273.15 °C) — where that energy bottoms out
    and no cooler temperature is defined.
  </p>
  <p>
    The expert edges of the phase map: water's solid–liquid boundary leans
    <strong>backwards</strong> (left-leaning) — squeeze ice and it melts, because liquid
    water is the denser phase; ice floats. Almost every other substance leans the other way,
    and nearly everything about lakes freezing from the top, frost heave, and glaciers
    grinding rock follows from that one reversed line. At the top end of the orange boundary
    sits the <strong>critical point</strong> (for water, 374 °C and 22 MPa): beyond it the
    liquid and gas phases become indistinguishable and merge into a <strong>supercritical
    fluid</strong> — dense like a liquid, flowing like a gas — which is what decaffeinates
    coffee beans industrially. And a fourth state waits above them all: <strong>plasma</strong>,
    matter so hot that electrons are torn from their atoms — the state of stars, lightning,
    neon signs and fusion reactors, and by a wide margin the most common state of ordinary
    matter in the universe, though the rarest in daily life.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Chemistry 2e (Liquids and Solids); Atkins, Physical Chemistry; Einstein, "Über die von der molekularkinetischen Theorie…" (1905).</p>
</TopicPage>
